## The Bug

**Line 11:** `fmt.Println(i)`

The goroutines capture a reference to the loop variable `i` (a closure over it), not a copy of its value at the time the goroutine was spawned. Since all five goroutines share the **same** variable `i`, by the time they actually execute, the `for` loop has already completed and `i` is `5`.

This is the classic "loop variable in goroutine" bug in Go ≤ 1.21. (Go 1.22 changed loop semantics so each iteration gets its own variable, which would fix this automatically — but you're on 1.21.)

## The Fix

Pass `i` as an argument to the goroutine so each one captures its own copy:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

